---@field labels? boxd.StringMap Machine labels. Keys: 1-63 chars of [a-z0-9._/-], starting and ending with [a-z0-9]; the `boxd/` prefix is reserved. Values: up to 256 chars, no control characters, may be empty. At most 64 per machine.
---@field spec? boxd.MachineSpec Create the machine from a machine spec: one resolved description of what it boots from, its size, labels, networks, isolation, egress, env and the integrations it needs. The machine keeps the spec (GetVmResponse `spec` and `created_spec`). A spec can't be combined with the fields it covers: `image_ref`, `team`, `labels`, `networks`, `isolated`, `env` and `config.vcpu`/`config.memory_bytes`; a create that sets one of them is refused, naming it. A spec whose base is a snapshot restores that snapshot, as CreateVmFromSnapshot does.
---@field deleteAfterSecs? string Delete-after timer the machine is born with, written together with the machine itself, so it never exists without the timer: permanently delete it `delete_after_secs` seconds of wall-clock time after it is created, or at the next match of `delete_after_cron` (UTC, e.g. "0 18 * * *"). Same rules and errors as SetDeleteAfter: one or the other, not both. Unset, zero or empty: no timer. Refused for a machine created for someone else (`spec.assign_to`): it is theirs to manage. Change or clear the timer later with SetDeleteAfter. The response's `machine` shows it (`delete_at_ms`, `delete_after_cron`). A server from before these fields ignores them and answers without `machine`: a client that gets no `machine` back sets the timer with SetDeleteAfter itself.
---@field deleteAfterCron? string
---@field os? string The guest OS: "" or "linux" (every machine so far), or "windows" (plans/2026-11-10-windows-guests.md): a firmware boot of the cluster's Windows image, which the operator registers. Needs a license with Windows guests (else PERMISSION_DENIED with a sentence to show), and in this release no `image_ref`, `config.volumes`, `spec` or team default snapshot (INVALID_ARGUMENT/FAILED_PRECONDITION). A Windows machine has no takeoff: no exec, cp or SSH through the proxy yet; RDP and the guest's own sshd through port forwards. It hibernates, and its snapshot is its disk alone (running, hibernated or stopped; a running one's crash-consistent), which CreateVmFromSnapshot boots as a new Windows machine (the snapshot decides the OS: leave `os` out of a restore). Its fork (ForkVm) is a copy of its disk too, generalized (its own SID) and booted cold as a new Windows machine, `starting` until Windows is up; its checkpoint is its disk alone, restored in place by a cold boot. The response's `machine.os` says what it boots. A server from before this field ignores it and creates a Linux machine: a client checks `machine.os` in the answer.

