---A machine spec, version 1: a declarative description of a machine. The SDKs build one from reusable pieces and send it resolved; the server checks every field, and an error names the field and the rule (`spec.size: ...`). Creating from a snapshot (a `snapshot` base, a team's default snapshot or CreateVmFromSnapshot) keeps the snapshot's spec and merges this one on top: maps merge with this spec winning, lists combine without duplicates, `isolated` only turns on, and `size` must match the snapshot's (a restore keeps its size). A fork keeps its source's spec. Either way the machine's stored spec lists the labels and networks the machine has, what it took from the snapshot or the source as well as its own.
---@class boxd.MachineSpec
---@field version? number Must be 1.
---@field base? boxd.MachineSpecBase Unset: the default image.
---@field size? string "<vcpu>x<memory GiB>", one of the cluster's sizes, e.g. "2x8". Empty: the org's default size (or, for a restore, the snapshot's).
---@field labels? boxd.StringMap Machine labels, same rules as CreateVmRequest.labels.
---@field networks? boxd.StringList Networks the machine joins; see CreateVmRequest.networks.
---@field isolated? boolean See CreateVmRequest.isolated.
---@field egress? boxd.StringList Egress allowlist the machine is born with: hosts, `*.` wildcards and public IPv4 addresses or ranges. Empty = unrestricted; `none` = nothing is reachable. See SetVmEgressAllow.
---@field env? boxd.StringMap Environment variables of this machine. They take precedence over every env var and secret of the same name set for the owner or the org.
---@field connections? boxd.StringMap Integrations the machine needs: toolkit name (e.g. "slack") to "personal" (the owner's own connection) or "shared:<tag>" (the org connection shared under that tag). Recorded on the machine; a missing connection never blocks the create.
---@field agents? boxd.StringList Agent logins the machine needs: "claude", "codex". Recorded on the machine.
---@field assignTo? string Create the machine for another member of the org: their user id. Only an owner or admin of the org may, in a create that names the org (`org`, a team, or an API key's org), with a login or a member API key (not an org key, not from inside a machine). The assignee must be a member of the org. The machine is theirs from the start: private to them, billed to the org, with their own integrations and agent logins; the creator can't reach it afterwards. GetVmResponse.created_by records the creator. Your own id is a plain create. Never inherited: a fork, a snapshot and a restore drop it, and a restore's spec may set its own.
---@field secrets? boxd.StringList Secrets the machine gets, by name. This is how a secret with scope "unattached" or "private-unattached" reaches a machine: such a secret reaches no machine on its own. A name resolves to your own secret in the org ("private-unattached" or "private") when you have one and the machine is yours, else to the org's ("unattached", "all" or "shared"). The machine keeps a reference, not a copy: changing the secret's value reaches it, deleting the secret stops it being injected, and setting it again with the same name brings it back. Rules: a name can't be in both `env` and `secrets`; only an owner or admin of the org may list an org "unattached" secret; a machine created for someone else (`assign_to`) or a shared machine only gets org secrets; an org "shared" secret only reaches shared machines. A fork, a snapshot and a restore resolve the inherited names again for the new machine with the same rules, and the create fails naming any that no longer resolve. Precedence, from the winner down: the machine's own (`env` and these secrets), the owner's private vars, the org's "shared" vars (shared machines only), the org's "all" vars.
---@field packages? boxd.StringList Shell commands run once, in order, as root, after the machine's first boot: e.g. "apt-get install -y nginx". Each runs with bash (sh when the image has none), `DEBIAN_FRONTEND=noninteractive`, no stdin and a 30-minute timeout; its output is appended to /var/log/boxd/packages.log in the machine. The first failure (a non-zero exit, a timeout) stops the rest; the machine stays usable. GetVmResponse.packages reports each one. At most 50 commands, each at most 4 KiB, no NUL bytes. Never run again: not on a reboot, a resume, a wake or a move. A restore marks done the commands that had run on the machine the snapshot was taken from and runs the snapshot spec's others, then its own; a fork keeps its source's report and runs nothing.

---What a machine spec boots from.
---@class boxd.MachineSpecBase
---@field team? string The default snapshot of this team (name or id). A team without one boots the default image in the team's org.
---@field snapshot? string A snapshot (name or id): the machine is a restore of it.

