------ Usage and cost --- Costs are priced at the org's CURRENT rates over the window, from the same per-machine meter the balance is charged from, so they track billing activity closely but are an estimate: billing activity (ListBillingActivity) is what was actually charged. Every money field is micro-units (1e-6) of `currency`. A window is unix seconds, [since, until). Usage is metered in 15-minute buckets, and a bucket counts when it starts inside the window. No window is the calendar month an org is invoiced for, so label totals with the window they cover. Rates the costs were priced at, micro-units of `currency` per unit-hour.
---@class boxd.UsageRates
---@field vcpuHourMicro string per vCPU-hour of CPU time used
---@field ramGibHourMicro string per GiB-hour of resident RAM (running or standby)
---@field diskGibHourMicro string per GiB-hour of used disk (every state)

---@class boxd.VarItem
---@field name string
---@field kind string
---@field scope string
---@field value string env only; always empty for secrets
---@field domains boxd.StringList secrets only; empty = not network-scoped
---@field boundMachines number Unattached secrets only: how many machines list it in their spec.

---@alias boxd.VarItemList boxd.VarItem[]

---VM configuration — all fields use 0/empty as "use default". Sizing (vcpu, memory_bytes) applies to FRESH creates only and must name one of the supported size classes - 1 vCPU · 4 GiB, 2 · 8, or 4 · 16 (naming one dimension resolves to its class; any other combination is refused). The pair is also validated against the billing org's quota ceiling: above it the create is refused (invalid_argument), never clamped. ForkVm and CreateVmFromSnapshot refuse non-zero sizing — a fork inherits its source's size, a restore is born at the size the snapshot was captured at.
---@class boxd.VmConfig
---@field vcpu? number 0 = the org's default; else one of the size classes below
---@field memoryBytes? string 0 = the org's default; else one of the size classes below
---@field diskBytes? string must be 0 (or exactly the fixed 100 GiB); per-machine disk sizing is refused
---@field srf? boxd.SrfConfig
---@field network? boxd.NetworkConfig
---@field volumes? boxd.VolumeMountList
---@field idleIgnoreOutbound? boolean Only traffic on connections opened TO the machine (SSH, HTTPS, a peer) resets the auto-suspend / auto-hibernate idle clock; connections the machine opened itself don't keep it awake. `optional`: set (true or false) is this machine's value. Unset means "not requested": a new machine takes its org's default (SetOrgIdleIgnoreOutboundDefault, the platform's setting when the org has none, off until an operator sets one), and a fork or restore-from-snapshot keeps the source's value. SetIdleIgnoreOutbound changes it afterwards. Was a plain bool in older versions: a client built against that never sends false on the wire, so it reads as unset here.

---Where a machine CAME FROM: a fork of another VM, or a restore of a snapshot. A standalone machine leaves this message UNSET rather than setting a kind that names its own absence, so a caller writes `if (source_info)` instead of `if (source_info.kind != "standalone")`. (The legacy `GetVmResponse.source` display string still reads "standalone" for those machines — it is unchanged.)
---@class boxd.VmSource
---@field kind string "fork" | "snapshot". Never "standalone": that is the unset message.
---@field name string The source VM's name (fork) or the snapshot's name (snapshot).
---@field version string The snapshot version. 0 for a fork.
---@field id string The source VM's id (fork) or the snapshot's id (snapshot). PROVENANCE, NOT A LIVE REFERENCE. A fork's parent may since have been destroyed and a snapshot may since have been deleted, so this id is not guaranteed to resolve — treat a lookup miss as normal, not as an error. Empty when the id is genuinely unrecoverable: a machine restored from a snapshot before the id was persisted (migration 0077) reports its name and version with an empty id, because `source_snapshot`'s "<name>:<version>" string cannot be resolved back to a row safely (snapshot names are unique per owner and can be re-created).

---One machine's usage over a window.
---@class boxd.VmUsage
---@field vmId string
---@field name string current name, or the last one seen if deleted
---@field owner string owner user_id; empty for machines no user owns
---@field ownerName string Human label for the owner, resolved server-side. Empty when nothing better than the user_id is known: fall back to `owner`.
---@field sharing string "shared" | "private"
---@field status string The machine's current status, or "deleted" once it no longer exists (its usage is kept for two years).
---@field vcpu number Size now (or when last seen, if deleted).
---@field memoryBytes string
---@field diskBytes string
---@field seconds? boxd.StateSeconds
---@field vcpuHours number What the cost is computed from, in the rates' units: vCPU-hours of CPU time used (a 2 vCPU machine busy 10% of an hour is 0.2), GiB-hours of resident RAM while running or in standby, GiB-hours of used disk in every state. cost_micro is these times `rates`, rounded up per component.
---@field ramGibHours number
---@field diskGibHours number
---@field costMicro string
---@field complete boolean All of this machine's usage in the window has been metered: the figures above won't grow. Metering lags by up to ~25 minutes, so a machine read right after it is deleted is not complete yet; one that still exists never is in a window that runs to now. Store a cost once this is true.

---@alias boxd.VmUsageList boxd.VmUsage[]

